a joint investigation by dw and more  than 40 media outlets has revealed how  uber aggressively lobbied governments  and potentially even skirted some laws  as it expanded its ride-sharing service  a trove of leaked documents suggests the  ride-sharing firm hid information from  investigators in the netherlands by  remotely killing internal devices in its  amsterdam office  the report also details the close  relationship between uber and then  french economy minister emmanuel macron  at a time when the company was trying to  avoid french regulations  macron gave the company direct access to  his staff  all right lisa lewis is our  correspondent in paris she joins me for  more on this  lisa i mean in some ways it's not  uncommon for european capitals to mix  very intensively into  their own industries and branches and  even pick favorite companies i mean  paris after all uh owns part of  carmichael renault so help me understand  what is the scandal here when it comes  to uber  well many people here feel it was not  morally acceptable for an economics  minister  to stand by the side of an international  company that they felt was a very  capitalistic and wanted to take down  french labor law that's indeed what some  opposition politicians said today they  said you know he really became  at the time economics minister as he  said he became the lobbyist for a  company that wanted to de-re regular  regulate french labor law and when you  look at it actually the situation of  french taxis and uber at the time was  and still is very different taxi drivers  about they're about 50 000 licenses in  this country and one license can cost up  to hundreds of thousands of years  whereas to become an uber driver  regulations are a lot a lot less strict  and they only have to pay a few hundred  euros and obviously people here were  rebelling against that and said french  prime minister french economics minister  at the time and now president emmanuel  macron should have done more to protect  french jobs and good jobs here because  they taxi drivers have the the  possibility of becoming employees uber  drivers are only self-employed  what have we heard from macron's camp  about what's been reported  iman macron has answered to the reports  he was absolutely contacted by those  carrying out the investigation he said  it was just normal to help some  companies that wanted to disrupt markets  to you know help them make  lift barriers so that they can operate  freely obviously at the time he was part  of a socialist government but even  socialist former president francois  hollande he had pledged to bring down  unemployment he actually said you can  hold me personally responsible in a  political way if unemployment doesn't go  down and that was obviously also what  emma macron was aiming at when he was  cooperating apparently or helping uber  to to develop the market here in france  he was hoping that thousands of jobs  could be created  lisa is the conflict over over uber as  explosive there today as it was years  ago when this was really a big issue  i think it kind of has calmed down  people here have eased into the  situation there's still a judicial  conflict going on actually such as in  other countries  caught here has decided in 2020 that  uber needed to give  it well employees that are not legal  employees at the moment the status of  employees and that they no longer had to  work as independent workers but that  decision still needs to be implemented  and it looks like it's very difficult to  get  either to implement that situation so  the the main focus of the conflict today  is on how these workers how the drivers  of uber can work here in france and what  conditions they would get as a worker  for ebirth  all right dw correspondent lisa lewis in  paris thank you very much  and for more i'm joined now by our  correspondent in brussels christine  mundo christine a high-ranking former eu  official is also implicated in these  leaks how embarrassing is this for the  european union  that's right uh monica a former official  by the name of neely cruz who was  effectively the eu's  digital chief at the time has been  implicated in in in these papers it's  been suggested that during her time  in in her role and in in the months  preceding that  that she was helping uber lobby uh with  senior executives within the european  union as well as in her home country the  netherlands papers show how she for  example was in full support of uber at  some point when the company had  some issues with the authorities in in  the city of brussels in her home country  that she was personally  not just out tweeting in support of uber  for example but also maneuvering behind  the scenes to get the regulators to  quote back off now for her part she  denies any wrongdoing saying that in her  position in her role it was her job to  be talking to to politicians about  companies like uber but if you look at  for example some exchanges in these very  leaks uh between uber executives it  appears that they were aware of the  sensitivity of this relationship that  that they had with this commissioner uh  on number of occasions instructing  colleagues to keep it  confidential so after leaving her post  we must note that she also then went on  to take a job at on uber's advisory  board where she was earning  something to the tune of two hundred  thousand dollars a year  now briefly if you would christine now  that this has been exposed what kind of  repercussions will there be  well transparency groups uh in in the  european union uh these are sort of ngos  are advocating for greater transparency  especially when it comes to what senior  officials do after leaving office the  eu's  ethics laws dictate that when  somebody leaves a position especially of  of that high  a position of a high nature is that that  they're meant to do what's called an  18-month cooling off period in that time  they're not meant to be lobbying  especially uh on topics that they were  previously responsible for in in the  case of nearly cruz it does appear that  it she might have been doing that in  that 18 mil period in that 18 month  period not only that she went on to  secure a job on uber's advisory board  after that so what these groups are  calling for is greater transparency they  say that the eu has egg on its face  because they've been warning  the commission about this because this  is not the first politician to find  herself in this position and they're  calling for greater tightening of the  rules when it comes to uh the ethics  breaches right christine wunder  reporting for us from brussels thank you  so much  you
 

